// Title: New Capture Session Launched Via DXCap.EXE
// Sigma ID: 60f16a96-db70-42eb-8f76-16763e333590
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Beyu Denis, oscd.community, Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2019-10-26 | Modified: 2022-06-09
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218
// Description: Detects the execution of "DXCap.EXE" with the "-c" flag, which allows a user to launch any
//   arbitrary binary or windows package through DXCap itself. This can be abused to potentially
//   bypass application whitelisting.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate execution of dxcap.exe by legitimate user
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/OtherMSBinaries/Dxcap/
//   - https://twitter.com/harr0ey/status/992008180904419328
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_dxcap_arbitrary_binary_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\DXCap.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "DXCap.exe") and ProcessCommandLine contains " -c "
